 "scope_id": "LOGIC-CIRCLE-20261107-001",
 "title": "Periodic logic makes polygons with rational pi",
 "subject": "Whether a periodic parity law can have a round circle of bond distance",
 "conclusion": "For a parity law on a doubly periodic plane graph, bond distance is twice the graph distance of the plane dual up to a constant, so its circle is half the dual's velocity polygon, with rational vertices and a rational pi (half its perimeter measured with its own distance). By Niven's theorem its only regular circles are the hexagon (pi = 3) and the parallelogram (pi = 4), and no periodic law has a round circle.",
